        <h2>Associated Tools</h2>
        <p>This database is part of a broader development framework involving the <b>Universities of Liège and Lille</b>, dedicated to the study and promotion of ceramics (and related materials).
            These tools complement — without replacing — the resources already available to researchers working in ceramic studies.</p>
        <h3>LOGACer</h3>
            <?php serve_picture_public("Logo-logacer-1.png", true, 'https://www.logacer.org', 70); ?>
        <p>LOGACer (<b>Logiciel Online pour la Gestion et l'Analyse de la Céramique</b> - Online Software for the Management and Analysis of Ceramics) was developed by Simon Dienst between 2025 and 2026. In order to make CeVICA available online, it was necessary to design a tool capable of efficiently presenting these data and their relationships.
            In its first version, this tool was inseparable from the database itself and was called "DBCer", with rapid development and non-institutional deployment beginning in 2024.</p>
        <p>In view of its identified potential, LOGACer underwent a complete rewrite as well as substantial improvements. The software, whose development is still ongoing, is becoming a comprehensive tool for sharing data (within a research team or with the scientific community) concerning pottery and glass, their illustrations, and analytical results.
            It is also gradually acquiring various analytical tools (particularly quantitative, statistical, and cartographic tools), thereby reducing the need to rely on external software. Designed to be easy to deploy and configurable, it is particularly suited to the study of artifacts from various sites within research projects that promote collaboration and Open Data.</p>
        <p>For more information, please visit the <a href='<?php echo create_url_proj(['logacer']); ?>' target='_blank'>dedicated LOGACer page</a>.</p>
        <h3>DBCer</h3>
            <?php serve_picture_public("Logo-dbcer-1.png", true, 'https://dbcer.huma-num.fr', 70); ?>
        <p>The former name of CeVICA/LOGACer, DBCer (<b>Ceramological Database</b>) is a simplified version of LOGACer. Its purpose is to enable the scientific community to make ceramic and glass inventories available.
            Unlike LOGACer, it is intended to be more generic and therefore:</p>
        <ul style="text-align:left;">
            <li>Brings together inventories from various geographical areas and research projects;</li>
            <li>Provides simplified inventory entry tools;</li>
            <li>Offers only basic browsing (without advanced search or cross-referencing) and the downloading of data and inventories;</li>
            <li>Allows for more granular privacy levels.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>DBCer was officially launched in September 2026 as part of a partnership between the University of Liège (Simon Dienst) and the University of Lille (Xavier Deru), within the Huma-Num TGIR.
        </p>
        <h4>ONICer</h4>
            <?php serve_picture_public("bandeau-logo-Onicer.png", true, 'https://www.onicer.org', 150); ?>
        <p>Developed in 2018–2019 and funded by the University of Lille under the direction of Xavier Deru, the <b>Outil Numérique pour l'Inventaire de la Céramique</b> (Digital Tool for Ceramic Inventory) was developed to facilitate, standardize, and systematize the recording of ceramics and glass.
            This application comprises several successive stages, from recording the project and context, to selecting technical and morphological classes, and finally entering counts. The application is accessible through a personal account where inventories are stored and can then be imported into personal databases. In addition to standardizing and facilitating the recording process, this systematic approach drastically reduces the risk of omissions and errors during this highly repetitive task.
            A brief example of its use is available <a href='https://youtu.be/f3v_hpofAdA' target='_blank'>here</a>, and the method is described in greater detail in an <a href='https://hdl.handle.net/2268/251242' target='_blank'>article</a> and on its <a href='https://www.onicer.org' target='_blank'>website</a>.</p>
            <p>Alongside this application, the conceptual framework, methodology, and reference-system metadata are freely available on the <a href='https://www.onicer.org' target='_blank'>www.onicer.org</a> website, hosted by Huma-Num.</p>
            <p>ONICer received the Research Promotion Award from the Maison européenne des Sciences de l'Homme.</p>
